Task: Verify contrast in both themes (Phase 1, home_page_rebuild_20260906)

WHAT CHANGED
Added scripts/verify-landing-contrast.mjs and two colour tokens the verification
proved were missing.

FILES
  A scripts/verify-landing-contrast.mjs
  M app/globals.css  (+--landing-muted-on-slab, +--landing-brass-on-slab, both themes)

WHY THE VERIFIER PARSES globals.css
It reads the --landing-* tokens back out of the committed stylesheet rather than
taking values from a hardcoded copy, so it measures what shipped instead of what was
intended. That is what caught both defects below; a design-time check against my own
numbers had passed.

TWO DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED
1. Light-theme muted text on the footer measured 2.16:1 against a 4.5:1 requirement.
   --landing-muted is #45474c, a dark grey sized for light grounds, and the footer is
   ink. The footer carries secondary text ('Founded 2021 / Sydney + Melbourne'), so
   this would have shipped illegible. Added --landing-muted-on-slab: 0.750 light /
   0.760 dark, now 9.01:1 and 9.45:1.

2. Brass eyebrow on the slab. --landing-brass-text (#775a19) gives only 3.11:1 on ink.
   It clears a 3:1 large-text threshold but the eyebrow is 12px uppercase, which needs
   4.5:1 — and the prototype uses the LIGHTER brass on its dark band anyway. Added
   --landing-brass-on-slab. The right value differs by theme, which is precisely what a
   token should absorb: 0.699 in light (the prototype's own brass, 7.44:1 on ink) but
   0.800 in dark, because the dark slab is RAISED to 0.268 and brass has to climb to
   stay legible against it. Now 7.44:1 light and 8.10:1 dark.

There are therefore three brass tokens, role-named rather than colour-named:
  --landing-brass          the CTA band surface
  --landing-brass-text     brass text on paper and warm grounds
  --landing-brass-on-slab  brass text on slab and footer grounds

